- **Format:** Traditional face-to-face
- **Software:** MATLAB

## Course Description

The course introduces statistical and computational methods used in modern astronomical data analysis, with an emphasis on gravitational-wave applications. It combines theoretical concepts with hands-on laboratory work in signal processing, probability and noise, detection and estimation, optimization, and machine learning.

## Main Topics

1. Basic signal processing
   - Analog and digital signals
   - Fourier transforms and FFTs
   - Sampling, aliasing, and digital filtering
   - Time-frequency analysis and spectrograms

2. Probability theory and noise
   - Random variables and probability distributions
   - Gaussian and colored noise
   - Power spectral density
   - Noise coloring and whitening

3. Detection and estimation
   - Maximum-likelihood estimation
   - Likelihood-ratio and generalized likelihood-ratio tests
   - Signal-to-noise ratio
   - Matched filtering

4. Optimization
   - Global-best and local-best particle swarm optimization
   - Optimization of gravitational-wave signal models

5. Machine learning
   - Supervised and unsupervised learning
   - Artificial neural networks
   - Support vector machines

